{"name":"Copper Cloud","hex":"#f4bb92","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"copper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A reddish-brown metallic chemical element (symbol Cu) with the atomic number 29; also, the metal made up of this element.","The reddish-brown colour of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1).","Having the reddish-brown colour of copper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/copper"},{"term":"cloud","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A visible mass of water droplets suspended in the air.","Any mass of dust, steam or smoke resembling such a mass."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become foggy or gloomy, or obscured from sight.","To overspread or hide with a cloud or clouds."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cloud"}],"entries":[{"title":"Copper","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a chemical element; it has symbol Cu and atomic number 29. It is a soft, malleable, and ductile metal with very high thermal and electrical conductivity. A freshly exposed surface of pure copper has a pinkish-orange color. Copper is used as a conductor of heat and electricity, as a building material, and as a constituent of various metal alloys, such as sterling silver used in jewelry, cupronickel used to make marine hardware and coins, and constantan used in strain gauges and thermocouples for temperature measurement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/NatCopper.jpg/330px-NatCopper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper"},{"title":"Cloud","blocks":[{"text":"In meteorology, a cloud is an aerosol consisting of a visible mass of miniature liquid droplets, ice crystals, or other particles, suspended in the atmosphere of a planetary body or similar space. Water, primarily, comprises the droplets and crystals. On Earth, clouds are formed as a result of saturation of the air when it is cooled to its dew point, or when it gains sufficient moisture, usually in the form of water vapor, from an adjacent source to raise the dew point to the ambient temperature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/ISS-40_Thunderheads_near_Borneo.jpg/330px-ISS-40_Thunderheads_near_Borneo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloud"},{"title":"Copper (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a reddish brown color that resembles the metal copper. The first recorded use of copper as a color name in English was in 1594."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Copper-18706.jpg/330px-Copper-18706.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Cu-Scheibe.JPG/500px-Cu-Scheibe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Cu-Scheibe.JPG/1280px-Cu-Scheibe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Cu-Scheibe.JPG/500px-Cu-Scheibe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A copper disc (99.95% pure) made by continuous casting; etched to reveal crystallites","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACu-Scheibe.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Molten_copper_in_bright_sunlight.gif/250px-Molten_copper_in_bright_sunlight.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Molten_copper_in_bright_sunlight.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https